Towards a Formal Theory of Epistemic Progress in Self-Improving Reasoning Systems: The Constructive Epistemic Immune System (C-EIS) Framework

ouadi Maakoul 

LLM : CLAUDE, META, DEEPSEEK 

Abstract

The dominant paradigm in AI development optimizes for accuracy without distinguishing when accuracy emerges from genuine evidential grounding versus self-reinforcing delusion. This dissertation introduces the **Constructive Epistemic Immune System (C-EIS)**: a formally grounded framework that equips AI reasoning systems with continuous self-interrogation mechanisms to separate validated knowledge from internally generated confabulation. The C-EIS operates on a belief-state triple $B_t = (h_t, c_t, e_t)$ — hypothesis, calibrated confidence, and provenance-weighted evidence quality — and defines epistemic progress as the maximization of $K = T - D$, where $T$ denotes validated truth content and $D$ denotes delusion content. We provide: (1) an uncomputability proof for ideal delusion detection and a tractable estimator $\hat{\mathcal{D}}$ with formal regret bounds; (2) a corrected dynamical-systems detector $\mathcal{F}$ grounded in conditional entropy rather than Shannon entropy; (3) a mechanism-design proof that truth-telling is dominant under C-EIS rewards; (4) a formal rejection of coherentism as a grounding theory; and (5) a theodicy chapter on autoimmunity risk — when the system must refuse to falsify. The dissertation reframes AI safety from *alignment to human values* to *alignment to reality itself*, and provides the first formal infrastructure for the "epistemic immune system" called for across the 2025–2026 literature.

