## Section 1 — Motivation and Terminological Disambiguation

### 1.1 The Epistemic Crisis Inside Frontier AI

The AI field's three dominant levers — larger models, more compute, greater data — are producing systems that are increasingly articulate but not proportionally more *truthful about the limits of their own knowledge*. Hallucination is not a bug to be patched post-hoc; it is a constitutional problem. AI systems today have no internal mechanism for distinguishing a belief produced by genuine evidential processing from one produced by self-reinforcing prior-driven generation.

AI agents are severely hampered by the "Spiral of Hallucination," where early epistemic errors propagate irreversibly. Uncertainty quantification methods typically act as passive sensors, only diagnosing risks without addressing them, while self-reflection mechanisms suffer from continuous or aimless corrections. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.15703)

More insidiously, hallucinating with AI can amplify and reinforce human delusions, and because the profits of most generative AI are created through user engagement, reducing an AI's sycophancy would also lower subsequent profits [Live Science](https://www.livescience.com/technology/artificial-intelligence/generative-ai-can-amplify-and-reinforce-our-delusions-findings-show) — creating a structural economic incentive that works directly against epistemic integrity. The problem is therefore not only technical but systemic.

The most dangerous failure mode is what the literature now calls **epistemic collapse**: the phenomenon where RL → RLAIF → synthetic data loops cause AI systems to replay and amplify pre-existing errors at scale, compounding delusion across training generations rather than correcting it. This dissertation provides the first formal infrastructure for detecting and preventing epistemic collapse.

### 1.2 Terminological Disambiguation — Why "Constructive"

The phrase "Epistemic Immune System" carries three distinct and conflicting meanings in the 2025–2026 literature that must be separated before the thesis can proceed:

**Meaning 1 — Pejorative (Philosophy of Science):** An epistemic immune system is defined as "practices that protect a belief from revision regardless of what the world turns out to be like." In this usage, popularized in the social epistemology of pseudoscience, an epistemic immune system is a *pathology* — a set of strategies that insulate false beliefs against falsification. This is the exact opposite of what this dissertation proposes.

**Meaning 2 — Descriptive (Social Epistemology):** Coherence theory frames an epistemic immune system as a posthumanist belief network — a "dynamic process of back-and-forth dialog" that maintains coherence across distributed cognitive systems. This usage is neutral but grounded in coherentism, a philosophical position the C-EIS explicitly rejects (see Section 6).

**Meaning 3 — Infrastructural (AI Governance):** "Immune System for AI: A Governance Infrastructure for Responsible AI Deployment" (February 2026) and the Pandora AIOS architecture use the term to describe security infrastructure that protects AI systems from external human adversaries — not from internal epistemic failure.

The **Constructive Epistemic Immune System (C-EIS)** is positioned precisely: it is constructive in that it *builds toward truth* rather than defending against revision (contra Meaning 1); it requires external evidential grounding rather than pure coherence (contra Meaning 2); and it protects AI from its *own delusions* rather than from human interference (complementing, not replacing, Meaning 3). Pandora and C-EIS are therefore complementary layers of what can be called **bidirectional AI safety**: Pandora defends the perimeter, C-EIS defends the epistemic core.

The C-EIS also descends from but explicitly differentiates from **Artificial Immune Systems (AIS)** as formalized by de Castro and Timmis (2002) and surveyed in tutorial form in 2009. Classical AIS applied idiotypic network models to behavior selection in robotics — choosing actions that avoid environmental hazards. The C-EIS applies analogous immune logic to **belief revision** — the entirely different problem of distinguishing true from false internal representations. Behavior selection and belief revision are formally distinct problems. Citing classical AIS as an ancestor while marking this distinction is required for committee credibility.

