## Section 6 — Mathematical Pillar IV: Dynamical Systems and the Corrected $\mathcal{F}$ Proof

### 6.1 The Proof Correction

The previous version of this dissertation used the Shannon-McMillan-Breiman theorem to argue that entropy of the chain-of-thought trace $H(\text{CoT}_t) \to 0$ as a detection signal for reasoning loops. This was incorrect: for a periodic chain, *unconditional* entropy does not approach zero — the chain continues to produce tokens with nonzero marginal entropy. What approaches zero is the **conditional entropy** — the entropy of the next step given all prior steps.

The corrected formulation:

$$\mathcal{F}(t) = H(\text{CoT}_{t+1} \mid \text{CoT}_{1:t})$$

We flag a loop when $\mathcal{F}(t) < \epsilon$ — when the next reasoning step becomes nearly perfectly predictable given the entire prior context.

### 6.2 Corrected Proof — Conditional Entropy Collapse Detects Loops

**Theorem:** For a periodic reasoning chain of period $L$, $H(\text{CoT}_{t+1} \mid \text{CoT}_{1:t}) \to 0$ as $t \to \infty$.

*Proof:* For a process with period $L$, $\text{CoT}_{t+1}$ is a deterministic function of $\text{CoT}_{t+1-L}$. As $t$ grows large enough that the full period $L$ is contained in the conditioning window $\{1:t\}$:

$$H(\text{CoT}_{t+1} \mid \text{CoT}_{1:t}) = H(\text{CoT}_{t+1} \mid \text{CoT}_{t+1-L}) = 0$$

since $\text{CoT}_{t+1}$ is determined with certainty by $\text{CoT}_{t+1-L}$ in a periodic chain. The conditional entropy converges to zero as the conditioning window grows to contain the full cycle. This is the correct application of the information-theoretic periodicity criterion: not unconditional entropy collapse, but *conditional* entropy collapse. $\square$

As reasoning chains are extended, models' attention to perceptual tokens decays and reliance on language and concept priors grows, leading to "reasoning drift" — a core mechanism for hallucination. LLMs reinforce false claims through self-reflection and hedging, causing error propagation even when corrections are introduced early. [Emergent Mind](https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/reasoning-driven-hallucination)

The conditional entropy formulation captures this precisely: reasoning drift is the state where $H(\text{CoT}_{t+1} \mid \text{CoT}_{1:t})$ is low not because the model is certain about the correct answer, but because it has become locked into a self-confirming trajectory.

### 6.3 Proof — Stochastic Perturbation Breaks Periodic Attractors

When $\mathcal{F}(t) < \epsilon$, the C-EIS injects perturbation noise $\eta_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2 I)$.

**Theorem:** Noise with variance $\sigma^2$ destabilizes a periodic orbit whose Jacobian eigenvalues satisfy $|\lambda_i| < 1 - \delta$.

*Proof:* Near periodic fixed point $h^*$ of period $L$, the linearized map is $Df^L(h^*)$. Perturbation variance evolves as:

$$\text{Var}(\delta h_t) = (Df^L)^2 \text{Var}(\delta h_{t-L}) + \sigma^2 I$$

For $\|\lambda_{\max}\| < 1-\delta$, the noise term dominates after:

$$t > \frac{\log(\sigma^2/\delta)}{\log(1/(1-\delta))}$$

steps, ejecting the trajectory from the attractor basin. This is the C-EIS *mutation mechanism*: stochastic perturbation analogous to biological immune-system randomization when a pathogen is detected. $\square$

### 6.4 Proof — Evaluator-Drift Bound

**Theorem:** $K$ remains $\epsilon$-interpretable under evaluator modification from $V_t$ to $V_{t+1}$ if:

$$\|V_{t+1} - V_t\|_{\text{Lip}} \leq \frac{\epsilon}{2}$$

*Proof:* For $L$-Lipschitz evaluators:

$$|V_{t+1}(B) - V_t(B)| \leq \|V_{t+1} - V_t\|_{\text{Lip}} \cdot \|B\|$$

With the Lipschitz bound, $|K_{V_{t+1}} - K_{V_t}| \leq \epsilon$ for bounded belief norms. Modifications violating the bound trigger rollback to the anchored benchmark — the non-modifiable cryptographic audit trail implementing the same integrity guarantee as hashchained audit logs in existing governance infrastructure. $\square$

The core challenge is how to tell real progress from self-serving metric drift when the system is allowed to change the ruler. This must be made analyzable even when the system is allowed to change not only the model but also the epistemic plumbing around the model. [Medium](https://medium.com/@omanyuk/when-recursive-self-improvement-changes-the-ruler-a-stability-theory-for-self-editing-ai-systems-2fb58064e87a)

**Reframing recursive self-improvement:** No architecture has ever demonstrated sustained, open-ended, autonomous self-improvement. Every experiment explicitly designed to test the hypothesis has produced the same pattern: modest gains over one or two cycles followed by rapid plateauing. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2512.04119) The C-EIS does not attempt to enable recursive self-improvement. It proves that *if* self-improvement occurs, it remains $K$-monotonic only while C-EIS is active. This reframes the existential-risk framing: C-EIS is a brake, not an accelerator.

