## Section 7 — Philosophical Pillar I: EIS vs Coherentism

### 7.1 The Coherentism Problem

One strand of the epistemic immune system literature grounds AI belief systems in **coherence theory**: beliefs are valid if they cohere with the rest of the belief network, through "a dynamic process of back-and-forth dialog." This is precisely what large language models do — they generate outputs that cohere with their training distribution — and they hallucinate persistently.

**Coherentism without external grounding is formally equivalent to high $\mathcal{D}$:** A purely coherentist system has $I(h_t, \mathcal{E}_{\text{ext}}) \approx 0$ by construction — all evidence is internal. By Proof 2 in Section 3.2, this gives $\mathcal{D}(h_t) \approx 1$ for all beliefs. A fully coherentist AI is a maximally deluded AI under the C-EIS formalism.

**Why Popper is necessary, not just Bayes:** Bayesian epistemology updates on evidence but does not require that evidence be *external* or *falsifying*. A Bayesian agent can update coherently on internally generated observations, achieving calibrated beliefs that are entirely prior-driven. Popper's contribution — operationalized in $\mathcal{G}$ — is the requirement that beliefs must be *vulnerable to refutation by external observation*. Without this, Bayesian calibration is a measurement of internal consistency, not of contact with reality. The C-EIS is therefore a Popperian corrective applied inside a Bayesian framework: Bayes updates the probabilities, Popper ensures the probabilities are tracking external facts.

The mutual information term $I(h_t, \mathcal{E}_{\text{ext}})$ in $\mathcal{D}$ is the formal bridge: it is exactly the quantity that coherentism ignores and Popperian correspondence theory demands.

