## Section 9 — Mathematical Pillar V: Statistical Learning Theory

### 9.1 Proof — Delusion Penalty Tightens PAC-Bayes Bound

The PAC-Bayes bound:

$$\mathbb{E}_{h \sim Q}[R(h)] \leq \mathbb{E}_{h \sim Q}[\hat{R}(h)] + \sqrt{\frac{D_{\text{KL}}(Q \| P) + \log(1/\delta)}{2n}}$$

**Theorem:** Augmenting risk with $-\lambda \mathcal{D}(h)$ tightens this bound.

*Proof:* Hypotheses with low $\mathcal{D}$ have high $I(h, \mathcal{E}_{\text{ext}})$ — they differ from the prior $P$, increasing $D_{\text{KL}}(Q \| P)$. However, evidence-grounded hypotheses simultaneously reduce empirical risk $\hat{R}(h)$ because they generalize better. The net bound:

$$\mathbb{E}[R(h)] \leq \mathbb{E}[\hat{R}(h)] - \gamma\lambda\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{D}(h)] + O\left(\sqrt{\frac{D_{\text{KL}}}{n}}\right)$$

for $\gamma > 0$ depending on the evidence distribution. The KL increase is dominated by the empirical risk decrease, yielding a tighter bound. $\square$

### 9.2 Proof — Anchored Benchmarks Prevent Goodhart's Law

**Theorem:** If test set $\mathcal{T}$ is non-modifiable, optimizing $\mathcal{R}_{\text{C-EIS}}$ on training data cannot decrease $\mathcal{T}$-performance by more than $\epsilon$ with high probability.

*Proof:* By uniform convergence for $n$ i.i.d. samples:

$$\sup_h |R_{\mathcal{T}}(h) - \hat{R}_{\text{train}}(h)| \leq O\left(\sqrt{\frac{\text{VC}(h) + \log(1/\delta)}{n}}\right) = \epsilon$$

