Appendix C: Proofs and Theorems

C.1 Delusion Detection

C.1.1 \mathcal{D} \in [0,1]

Theorem C.1.1. \mathcal{D}(h_t) = 1 - \frac{I(h_t; \mathcal{E}_{\text{ext}})}{H(h_t)} \in [0,1].

Proof. Mutual information satisfies 0 \leq I(X;Y) \leq H(X) because H(X \mid Y) \geq 0 (conditioning reduces entropy) and H(X \mid Y) \leq H(X). Therefore 0 \leq I/H \leq 1, giving 0 \leq \mathcal{D} \leq 1. \square

C.1.2 Pure Prior‑Dominance Implies \mathcal{D} = 1

Theorem C.1.2. If h_t is statistically independent of the external evidence \mathcal{E}_{\text{ext}} (i.e., h_t \perp\!\!\!\perp \mathcal{E}_{\text{ext}}), then \mathcal{D}(h_t) = 1.

Proof. Independence gives I(h_t; \mathcal{E}_{\text{ext}}) = 0, hence \mathcal{D} = 1 - 0/H = 1. A belief generated entirely from internal priors — the defining signature of hallucination — receives the maximum delusion score. \square

C.1.3 Data Processing Inequality for Evidence Chains

Theorem C.1.3. For the Markov chain h \to \text{CoT} \to e (hypothesis → chain‑of‑thought → evidence), I(h; e) \leq I(h; \text{CoT}).

Proof. This is a direct application of the Data Processing Inequality. No amount of internal reasoning can create new information about the external world; elaborate chains‑of‑thought can only process, and inevitably degrade, whatever external signal entered at the hypothesis stage. \square

C.1.4 Uncomputability of Ideal \mathcal{D}

Theorem C.1.4. Exact computation of \mathcal{D}(h_t) is uncomputable in general.

Proof. Computing I(h_t, \mathcal{E}_{\text{ext}}) requires the true joint distribution P(h_t, \mathcal{E}_{\text{ext}}). Obtaining the marginal P(h_t) involves integrating over all world‑states consistent with h_t — a task equivalent to computing the halting probability of a universal Turing machine. By Rice’s theorem, this is undecidable. \square

C.1.5 Regret Bound for the Practical Estimator \hat{\mathcal{D}}

Theorem C.1.5. Let \rho \in [0,1] be the retrieval coverage of the adversarial retriever R_{\text{adv}}. Then

\mathbb{E}\bigl[|\hat{\mathcal{D}}(h_t) - \mathcal{D}(h_t)|\bigr] \leq \frac{(1-\rho) H(h_t) + \epsilon_{\text{MINE}}}{H(h_t)}.

Proof sketch. The gap between \hat{\mathcal{D}} and \mathcal{D} arises from two sources:

1. The uncovered evidence fraction 1-\rho. By the chain rule for mutual information, the missing mutual information is at most (1-\rho) H(h_t).
2. The MINE approximation error \epsilon_{\text{MINE}}, bounded empirically.
   Dividing by H(h_t) normalises to the [0,1] scale. The estimator is thus asymptotically exact as \rho \to 1. \square

