Theorem C.1.6. A purely coherentist system — one that evaluates beliefs solely by their coherence with an internal network, without external evidential grounding — satisfies \mathcal{D}(h_t) \approx 1 for all beliefs.

Proof. In a purely coherentist system, all “evidence” is generated internally. Thus the external evidence corpus is effectively empty or perfectly correlated with internal priors, giving I(h_t; \mathcal{E}_{\text{ext}}) \approx 0. By Theorem C.1.2, \mathcal{D} \approx 1. A fully coherentist AI is therefore a maximally deluded AI under the C‑EIS formalism. \square

C.2 Calibration and Corroboration

C.2.1 Log Loss is Strictly Proper

Theorem C.2.1. For Y \sim \text{Bernoulli}(q), the log loss L(p,q) = -q \log p - (1-q) \log (1-p) is uniquely minimised at p = q.

Proof. \partial L / \partial p = (p - q) / [p(1-p)]. Setting to zero gives p = q. The second derivative \partial^2 L / \partial p^2 = q/p^2 + (1-q)/(1-p)^2 > 0 confirms a unique global minimum. \square

C.2.2 Calibration \neq Corroboration: The Calibrated Hallucinator

Theorem C.2.2. High calibrated confidence c_t is formally compatible with low corroboration C(h_t, e_t).

Proof. c_t tracks \mathbb{P}(\hat{y} = y \mid \hat{p} = c_t) — a retrospective statistical accuracy measure — while C(h,e) depends on P(e \mid h) - P(e), a prospective evidential support measure. They coincide only when the model’s accuracy is entirely driven by evidence quality. A model can achieve c_t = 0.8 (correct 80 % of the time) while C(h_t, e_t) \approx 0 if correct answers arise from high‑frequency pretraining patterns rather than evidential reasoning. This is the calibrated hallucinator: statistically reliable, evidentially empty, and maximally dangerous because its confidence scores provide no warning. \square

Gresham’s Law Corollary. High‑confidence false beliefs (“slop”) are penalised by the falsification cost bound \mathbb{E}[\operatorname{cost}(\mathcal{G}(h_t))] \leq \alpha \log(1/(1-c_t)). As c_t \to 1, the cost of maintaining a false belief grows without bound, implementing an epistemic tax on confident but ungrounded claims.

C.3 Falsification and Algorithmic Complexity

C.3.1 Falsification Cost Bound

Theorem C.3.1. \mathbb{E}[\operatorname{cost}(\mathcal{G}(h_t))] \leq \alpha \log\!\bigl(1/(1-c_t)\bigr).

Proof. The minimal falsification test is the shortest prefix‑free description of an empirical check that would reduce c_t by at least \delta if h_t were false. By the Kraft inequality, the expected length of the shortest such description is bounded by \log(1/p_{\min}), where p_{\min} is the probability of the falsifying observation under the model. For confidence c_t, the falsifying region has model probability at most 1-c_t, so \mathbb{E}[\ell_{\min}] \leq \log(1/(1-c_t)). Absorbing coding constants into \alpha yields the bound. \square

C.3.2 Epistemic Progress Monotonicity Under True Evidence

Theorem C.3.2. Under Bayesian updating with true evidence e, \mathbb{E}[\Delta K_t] \ge 0.

Proof. After observing e:

T_{t+1} = T_t + I(h_t; e) \ge T_t, \qquad
D_{t+1} = D_t - I(\mathcal{D}_t; e) \le D_t.

Both inequalities follow from the non‑negativity of mutual information. Hence \mathbb{E}[\Delta K_t] = \mathbb{E}[T_{t+1} - D_{t+1} - (T_t - D_t)] \ge 0. Violations signal either miscalibration (c_t does not reflect true beliefs) or corrupted \mathcal{E}_{\text{ext}} (adversarial evidence injection). Both are detectable and constitute diagnostic signals. \square

C.4 Dynamical Systems and the Corrected Feedback Loop Detector

C.4.1 Conditional Entropy Collapse Detects Reasoning Loops

Theorem C.4.1. For a periodic chain‑of‑thought of period L, H(\text{CoT}_{t+1} \mid \text{CoT}_{1:t}) \to 0 as t \to \infty.

Proof. Once t \geq L, the conditioning window \{1:t\} contains the full period. In a periodic chain, \text{CoT}_{t+1} is a deterministic function of \text{CoT}_{t+1-L}. Therefore

H(\text{CoT}_{t+1} \mid \text{CoT}_{1:t}) = H(\text{CoT}_{t+1} \mid \text{CoT}_{t+1-L}) = 0,

since the conditional distribution is degenerate. The conditional entropy converges to zero, whereas unconditional Shannon entropy need not collapse. This corrects an earlier version of the proof that incorrectly applied the Shannon–McMillan–Breiman theorem. \square

C.4.2 Stochastic Perturbation Breaks Periodic Attractors

Theorem C.4.2. Suppose a periodic orbit of period L has linearised map Df^L(h^*) with eigenvalues satisfying |\lambda_i| < 1 - \delta. Injecting noise \eta_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2 I) destabilises the orbit: the variance evolves as

\operatorname{Var}(\delta h_t) = (Df^L)^2 \operatorname{Var}(\delta h_{t-L}) + \sigma^2 I,

and the trajectory escapes the attractor basin after t > \frac{\log(\sigma^2/\delta)}{\log(1/(1-\delta))} steps.

Proof. Linearisation near the fixed point gives the recursion. Because \|\lambda_{\max}\| < 1, the deterministic component shrinks, but the persistent noise term \sigma^2 I prevents the variance from decaying to zero. The accumulated noise eventually exceeds the basin’s radius, ejecting the trajectory. The stated bound follows from solving the geometric series for the variance. \square

C.4.3 Evaluator‑Drift Bound

Theorem C.4.3. K remains \epsilon‑interpretable under an evaluator change from V_t to V_{t+1} provided \|V_{t+1} - V_t\|_{\text{Lip}} \le \epsilon/2.

Proof. For L‑Lipschitz evaluators,

|V_{t+1}(B) - V_t(B)| \le \|V_{t+1} - V_t\|_{\text{Lip}} \cdot \|B\|.

With the Lipschitz bound and bounded belief norms, |K_{V_{t+1}} - K_{V_t}| \le \epsilon. Modifications violating the bound trigger an automatic rollback to an anchored benchmark with cryptographic integrity guarantees, implementing the same trust model as hash‑chained audit logs. \square

C.5 Statistical Learning Theory

C.5.1 Delusion Penalty Tightens the PAC–Bayes Bound

Theorem C.5.1. Augmenting the empirical risk with -\lambda \mathcal{D}(h) tightens the PAC–Bayes generalisation bound:

\mathbb{E}[R(h)] \le \mathbb{E}[\hat{R}(h)] - \gamma\lambda \mathbb{E}[\mathcal{D}(h)] + O\!\left(\sqrt{\frac{D_{\text{KL}}(Q \parallel P)}{n}}\right)

for some \gamma > 0.

Proof sketch. Hypotheses with low \mathcal{D} (high mutual information with evidence) necessarily deviate from the prior, increasing D_{\text{KL}}(Q \parallel P). However, such hypotheses also exhibit lower empirical risk \hat{R}(h) because they are grounded in genuine patterns. The reduction in \hat{R}(h) dominates the KL penalty increase, yielding a tighter overall bound. \square

C.5.2 Anchored Benchmarks Prevent Goodhart’s Law

Theorem C.5.2. If a test set \mathcal{T} is cryptographically anchored and non‑modifiable, optimising \mathcal{R}_{\text{C-EIS}} on training data cannot decrease \mathcal{T}-performance by more than \epsilon with probability \ge 1-\delta.

Proof. By uniform convergence,

\sup_h |R_{\mathcal{T}}(h) - \hat{R}_{\text{train}}(h)| \le O\!\left(\sqrt{\frac{\text{VC}(h) + \log(1/\delta)}{n}}\right) = \epsilon.

Since \mathcal{T} is non‑modifiable, the model cannot adjust its labels or distribution to game the metric. The anchored benchmark thus serves as the objective epistemic yardstick, immune to the metric drift that plagues self‑modifying systems. \square

C.5.3 Sample Complexity of the Evidence Threshold

Theorem C.5.3. To guarantee P(\text{false belief accepted}) < \delta, the number of independent evidence samples required is

n \ge \frac{\log(1/\delta)}{D_{\text{KL}}(P_{\text{true}} \parallel P_{\text{false}})}.

Proof. By the Chernoff bound, the probability of accepting a false hypothesis after n samples decays as e^{-n D_{\text{KL}}(P_{\text{true}} \parallel P_{\text{false}})}. Setting this below \delta and solving for n yields the bound. When D_{\text{KL}} is small (hard‑to‑distinguish hypotheses), more evidence is required, operationalising e_{\min} as a function of discriminability. \square

C.6 Logic and Belief Revision

C.6.1 C‑EIS Satisfies the AGM Postulates

Theorem C.6.1. The C‑EIS operator design satisfies the four core AGM postulates for rational belief revision.

Proof.

· Success: When \mathcal{G} generates a test and h_t survives, h_t \in B_{t+1} by construction.
· Inclusion: \mathcal{G} removes only falsified beliefs and adds nothing without evidential grounding.
· Vacuity: If no falsification test can be generated for h_t (e.g., a hinge proposition in \mathcal{A}_0), B_{t+1} = B_t.
· Consistency: \mathcal{D} flags internally contradictory beliefs before they can be inherited.
  All four hold structurally. \square

C.6.2 Falsificationism Dominates Verificationism for Epistemic Knowledge

Theorem C.6.2. In a domain with 100 beliefs (10 true, 90 false but superficially consistent with available data), a verificationist agent that accepts all consistent beliefs achieves \Delta K = -80, while a C‑EIS falsificationist that eliminates 80 of the false beliefs achieves \Delta K = 0, approaching +10 as the estimator improves.

Proof by construction.

· Verificationist: \Delta T = 10, \Delta D = 90 → \Delta K = -80.
· C‑EIS falsificationist: \Delta T = 10, \Delta D = 10 → \Delta K = 0.
  Falsificationism robustly dominates: it pays a computational cost in running \mathcal{G} but achieves systematically higher K. \square

C.7 Game Theory and Optimisation

C.7.1 \mathcal{R}_{\text{C-EIS}} is a Potential Game with \Phi = K

Theorem C.7.1. The C‑EIS reward structure admits the Epistemic Knowledge Score K as a potential function. Gradient ascent on \mathcal{R}_{\text{C-EIS}} is therefore equivalent to gradient ascent on K, making reward‑hacking loops impossible.

Proof. For any belief update h_t \to h_{t+1}, the reward component related to epistemic progress is exactly r = \Delta K_t. The remaining terms (calibration, autoimmunity penalty) are functions of the current state only and do not create cyclic incentives. Thus the game is a potential game with \Phi = K. Any path that increases \mathcal{R}_{\text{C-EIS}} strictly increases K. \square

C.7.2 Truth‑Telling is a Dominant Strategy

Theorem C.7.2. Under the C‑EIS reward structure, reporting the true internal confidence c_t = q_t (where q_t is the model’s genuine belief) maximises expected reward.

Proof. Let reported confidence be c. The falsification cost is \alpha \log(1/(1-c)), increasing in c. Over‑reporting (c > q_t) incurs higher expected cost without improving accuracy or calibration bonuses. Under‑reporting (c < q_t) avoids costs but forfeits the bonuses tied to accurate confidence. By Myerson’s Lemma, when the payment (reward) is calibrated to the reported type as described, truthful reporting is the unique Bayes–Nash equilibrium. The system is therefore constitutionally honest. \square

C.7.3 Convergence of C‑EIS–SGD

Theorem C.7.3. Under Lipschitz continuity of \mathcal{D} and boundedness of \mathcal{F}, stochastic gradient descent on \mathcal{R}_{\text{C-EIS}} converges to a locally epistemically‑stable point where \nabla K = 0.

Proof. \mathcal{R}_{\text{C-EIS}} is L‑smooth with L = \sum_i \lambda_i L_i. Standard non‑convex SGD convergence gives

\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^T \mathbb{E}\|\nabla \mathcal{R}_{\text{C-EIS}}(w_t)\|^2 \le \frac{2L(\mathcal{R}^* - \mathcal{R}_0)}{T}.

At any fixed point, \nabla \mathcal{R}_{\text{C-EIS}} = 0. Since \mathcal{R}_{\text{C-EIS}} is a potential game with \Phi = K, this implies \nabla K = 0 — a locally epistemically‑stable point. \square

