## Chapter 7 — Terminological and Positioning Verification

### Step 7.1 — Systematic Literature Disambiguation

The term "Epistemic Immune System" carries three existing meanings in the literature — the pejorative philosophical use, the social epistemology use, and the AI governance infrastructure use — as identified in Document 38. Before any conference submission, a future systematic literature review must:

- Map all existing uses of the term across PhilArchive, arXiv, Zenodo, and ACL Anthology
- Confirm that "Constructive EIS" or "Formal EIS" does not itself collide with existing terminology
- Position the dissertation's definition relative to the Pandora AIOS architecture and the Artificial Immune Systems literature from 2009 onward

This is not experimental but it is essential: a terminology collision at a committee defense is a structural failure, not a content failure.

### Step 7.2 — $e_t$ Provenance Weighting Implementation Specification

The dissertation defines $e_t$ as a structured evidence quality tensor. The future implementation step requires formalizing:

$$e_t = \sum_i w_i \cdot \text{indep}(s_i)$$

where $s_i$ are retrieved sources and $w_i$ are weights from retrieval diversity scores. Before any empirical test, the weighting function $w_i$ must be specified precisely enough that two independent implementers produce the same $e_t$ scores on the same input — a reproducibility requirement that is standard in experimental science but often neglected in theoretical AI work.

